# EXAMPLE ONLY -- this file is NOT shipped in the image and is NOT read by
# anything. It is the shape of the file plus the reasoning behind it.
#
# Every address, MAC and router-id below has been replaced by a placeholder.
# Put your own in
#
#     /mnt/data/config/network.conf
#
# which lives on the data partition, so it survives an upgrade, a rollback and
# a reinstall -- and is never overwritten by an image built for this model.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE USED TO CARRY ONE PHYSICAL SWITCH'S IDENTITY, IN A PUBLIC REPO.
# An image built from it made a second switch a duplicate of the first: same
# management address, same MAC, same OSPF router-id in the same area. Keep real
# values in <name>.site.conf, which .gitignore excludes, or on the switch.
#
# Network configuration for this board, copied from the EdgeNOS installation
# it replaces so the two can be compared directly.
#
# Captured from the running switch on 2026-08-28. The et* ports are front-panel
# and exist only once the datapath is up; a management-plane boot brings up
# eth0 and says plainly that the others are absent, rather than failing.
#
#   vrf <name> table <n>
#   iface <name> <address/prefix> [mtu <n>] [mac <addr>] [vrf <name>]
#   route <prefix|default> via <gateway> [dev <iface>] [vrf <name>]

# Management. The BCM57762 the kernel drives with tg3 -- a normal NIC, present
# whether or not the ASIC is.
# The MAC is stated here because this board does not keep it anywhere tg3 can
# find it -- it lives in board data on an i2c SEEPROM, which nothing in NOSaic
# reads yet. Read off the switch from Aboot's own ifconfig on ma1.
#
# This is a stopgap and should disappear: once the prefdl reader exists, the
# board supplies its own address and no configuration file should be asserting
# hardware identity.
# The management VRF. eth0 and its routes live in table 1001, not the main
# table the datapath mirrors into the chip and routes transit traffic through.
# ssh answers on eth0 anyway: apply-network.sh turns tcp_l3mdev_accept on, so
# services listening in the default VRF accept connections from it. Needs a
# kernel with CONFIG_NET_VRF, which is outside the A/B slot -- see
# recipes/linux/config/common.fragment.
vrf mgmt table 1001
iface eth0 <address>/<prefix> mac <this switch's MAC> vrf mgmt
route default via <address> vrf mgmt

# The loopback carries the OSPF router-id. It is a loopback precisely so the
# id does not move when a front-panel port goes down.
iface lo <address>/<prefix>

# et1 and et2 are gone, and were <address>/<prefix> and .<host>/<prefix>.
#
# They came over from the EdgeNOS capture and described links that do not
# exist here: both ports were admin up with an address and no carrier, and
# neither far end -- <address> and .58 -- has ever answered. A switch
# carrying addresses for links that are not there is a switch whose
# configuration lies about the topology, and it lies in the most expensive
# direction: `show ports` and `ip addr` both look like a cabling fault on two
# ports that are simply not cabled.
#
# They also cost more than tidiness. Each /29 is advertised into OSPF the
# moment FRR sees an address on an up interface, so the area carried two
# prefixes no traffic could ever reach.
#
# If either link is ever cabled, the addresses are in this comment and in the
# git history. Putting them back is one line each; leaving them in was a
# permanent claim that the links existed.

# The two 40G cages. Each /29 follows the same one-subnet-per-link pattern
# as the 10G links above; .<host>/<prefix> and .<host>/<prefix> were the next free blocks.
#
#   et54  <->  AS5610 swp51        (<address> on the far side)
#   et53  <->  7050TX-64 port 50   (far side not yet configured)
#
# Configured here and NOT yet carrying traffic. Both cages link at 40G and
# stay up, both far ends agree, and neither has passed a frame: the port's
# MAC counts nothing in either direction while its PCS reports link. That
# is an open fault, written up in docs/todo.md. These lines are here
# because the addressing is settled and correct; when the datapath fault is
# fixed the links come up with no further configuration.
# et52 <-> 7050TX-64 port 49. The next free /29 after et53's .<host>/<prefix>; this box
# takes the lower address and the far side .82, the same way round as et53 and
# et54.
iface et52 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface et53 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface et54 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600

# No "pin" for the build network any more. It was a static route out of
# eth0, there because OSPF learned the management subnet over the front panel
# and the box's own replies followed it through the CPU punt path. With eth0 in
# the management VRF those replies are routed in table 1001, where OSPF's
# routes never go. Traffic the switch ORIGINATES in the default VRF (an scp
# from the switch, say) still follows the main table.

# IPv6 on the management port.
#
# A second iface line for the same interface is how a second address is
# stated: apply_ifaces reads every line in the file and adds each address it
# names, and the already-correct test is per address, so re-running stays free.
# The MAC is not repeated here -- it is set by the line above, and a second
# place to state it is a second place for it to drift.
iface eth0 <ipv6-address>/<prefix> vrf mgmt
route default via <ipv6-address> vrf mgmt

# ── VLANs and routed VLAN interfaces ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. A port is routed until a switchport line puts it in a VLAN, and
# routed again when it leaves the last one. The routed VLAN interface is
# vlan<VID>, and an iface line on it creates it. docs/vlan.md has the model.
#
#   vlan <vid>
#   switchport <port> access <vid>
#   switchport <port> trunk <vid>[,<vid>...] [native <vid>]
#   iface vlan<vid> <address>/<prefix> [mtu <n>]
#
# Reserved on this board -- the datapath's per-port VLANs: 1001-1054.
#
# vlan 10
# vlan 20
# switchport et1 access 10
# switchport et52 trunk 10,20 native 1
# iface vlan10 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
